               <msContents>
                  <summary>Shlomoh ben Shabtai's commentary on the <hi rend="italic">Sheʾiltot</hi>
                     by Rabbi Aḥai Gaon</summary>
                  <msItem>
                     <title type="desc" xml:lang="he" key="work_3380">פרוש שאלתות של רב אחאי
                        גאון</title>
                     <title type="desc" xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3380">Perush Sheʾiltot
                        shel Rav Aḥai Gaon</title>
                     <author key="person_7039">
                        <persName>Shlomoh ben Shabtai</persName>
                        <persName xml:lang="he">שלמה בן שבתי</persName>
                     </author>
                     <author key="person_7092">
                        <persName>Judah ben Benjamin Anav</persName>
                        <persName xml:lang="he">יהודה בן בנימין ענו</persName>
                     </author>
                     <note>The author quotes the <hi rend="italic">Arukh</hi> as the latest
                        authority, and employs Italian words.</note>
                     <note>Up to the second <hi rend="italic">Sheʾilta</hi> of <hi rend="italic"
                           >parashat ki tavo</hi> (<locus>f. 77v</locus>), the commentary is by
                           <persName key="person_7039">Shlomoh ben Shabtai</persName>; from this
                        point on until the end, the commentary is that of <persName
                           key="person_7092">Judah ben Benjamin Anav</persName>. This change in the
                        commentary text is signalled by the scribe on <locus>ff. 77v,
                        90v</locus>.</note>
                     <note>One of the scribes, <persName key="person_7094">Binyamin ben Avraham
                           ha-Rofe <foreign xml:lang="he">בנימין בן אברהם
                        הרופא</foreign></persName>, added his own comments which are integrated
                        within the main text block. Some of his comments are signed <foreign
                           xml:lang="he">צעיר רודם</foreign>, which is likely to be a circumlocution
                        for the name Binyamin (Cf. Psalm 68:28).</note>
                     <note>The <hi rend="italic">Sheʾiltot</hi> after the Pentateuch differ from the
                        edition.</note>
                     <note>At the end of the manuscript there are few short texts, headed <foreign
                           xml:lang="he">מילי חסידותא</foreign> and copied also by the scribe
                           <persName>Binyamin ben Avraham ha-Rofe</persName>. These include a list
                        of Tannaʾim and Amoraʾim (<locus>ff. 90v-92r</locus>), a short treatise on
                        the practice of <hi rend="italic">nikkur ha-basar</hi> attributed to
                           '<persName>Rabbenu Shlomoh <foreign xml:lang="he">רבינו שלומה
                              זצוק״ל</foreign></persName>' (probably <persName key="person_19684048"
                           >Rashi</persName>) (<locus>ff. 92r-92v</locus>); and some kabbalistic
                        rules on the writing of Hebrew letters in ritual scrolls (<locus>f.
                           92v</locus>).</note>
                     <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
